      'Ghost Controls openers are DC units designed around a battery, so the battery’s health decides how the gate behaves. On Celina acreage, where a gate can sit far from the house with no power at the road, that matters a great deal. A battery losing capacity reads as a weak opener: slow travel, partial opening, a remote that works one day and not the next. Solar output falls when panels collect dust or lose their aim. Long farm gates on shifting clay also bind at the hinge, and that extra effort shortens battery life and wears the arm gears.',
      'On a Ghost Controls TDS2 or TSS1 we always test the charging system before anything else. That means a load test on the battery, a measurement of what the panel is actually delivering, and a check of the control box and receiver. If the gate drags, the hinges and posts get attention, because a free-moving leaf is easier on every part. Arm gear wear, bracket movement, auto-close timer faults and remote or keypad pairing loss round out the usual list, and each is repairable without replacing the whole opener.',
